title: Fractured Identity Cube Drafts
date: 09-11-2026
tags: magic, cube

# Jonesing for Jace

Fractured Identity! A love letter to Magic and, especially, it's mopey main man Jace.

Empower Jace is actually a pretty sweet mechanic for booster draft, but at this point, I've seen enough of Jace to last a lifetime. That said, this set added a ton of other great cards.

How do they play? Let's find out!

# Trophy Dimir Reani-midrange
![Trophy Dimir Midrange](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-dimir-midrange.png)

I see an [Archon](https://scryfall.com/card/frc/47/archon-of-cruelty), I take an Archon.

We're very light on renimation here and skewing closer towards a more general midrange deck. Actually, with another evasive creature or two, I would've been happy to run the [Fallen Shinobi](https://scryfall.com/card/otc/226/fallen-shinobi) over the Archon.

Still, happy to bring back a relatively fair [Wan](https://scryfall.com/card/tle/98/wan-shi-tong-all-knowing) or [Oc](https://scryfall.com/card/dsk/42/abhorrent-oculus) and the [Frog](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/199/psychic-frog) is going to be great anywhere.

# Trophy Azorius Midrange
![Trophy Azorius Midrange](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-azorius-tempo.png)

A fairly fair blue/white tempo deck, which I'm always a sucker for. This skewed white enough the [Force](https://scryfall.com/card/2x2/50/force-of-negation) was a stretch, but definitely worth the blue.

We ended up super scarce on fixing, though double mox helped make up for that. I would've loved to run the [Adeline](https://scryfall.com/card/tdc/108/adeline-resplendent-cathar) or the [Figure](https://scryfall.com/card/ecl/224/figure-of-fable) in the board if they weren't so tough on the mana.

# Trophy Dimir Midrange
![Trophy Dimir Midrange](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-dimir-owl-reanimator.png)

What did I say about taking the Archon?

A second Dimir midrange deck, "splashing" the reanimation. This time we did get to run the Shinobi though! [Strix](https://scryfall.com/card/otc/215/baleful-strix) and [Borrower](https://scryfall.com/card/soc/190/brazen-borrower-petty-theft) give us enough evasion to squeak through.

I think I've mentioned before, I've been pleasantly surprised by [Superiod Spider-Man](https://scryfall.com/card/spm/155/superior-spider-man). Though I wouldn't go out of my way for it exactly, it's a nice pickup for a blue/black with some good creatures. Stealing something juicy out of the opponent's bin really pushes it over the edge.

# Trophy Boros Beauty
![Trophy Boros Beauty](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-boros-beauty.png)

Basically a perfect Boros agro deck.

Almost unstoppable, though I did throw the first game by keeping a no-land mox hand into the opponent's [Inquisition](https://scryfall.com/card/2x2/80/inquisition-of-kozilek). Whoops, immediate shame concede!

Like you can see from the sideboard, we had our pick of the litter. New card [Samut](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/251/samut-hazorets-champion) was one of the last cuts. Feels like it's a step below the premium threats as basically a French vanilla creature, but then hasting a [Fable](https://scryfall.com/card/neo/141/fable-of-the-mirror-breaker-reflection-of-kiki-jiki) token or something seems like it'd be strong. Dunno!

# Trophy Jund Reani-midrange
![Trophy Jund Reanimidrange](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-jund-reanimator-midrange.png)

Back to our precious Jund midrange decks. Hey, there's that [Archon](https://scryfall.com/card/frc/47/archon-of-cruelty) again!

This deck was a treat, exactly what I want to be playing. [Barrogoyf](https://scryfall.com/card/plst/M3C-102/barrowgoyf), [Fable](https://scryfall.com/card/neo/141/fable-of-the-mirror-breaker-reflection-of-kiki-jiki), [Grist](https://scryfall.com/card/dsc/220/grist-the-hunger-tide), even a cheeky [Bloodbraid](https://scryfall.com/card/m3c/70/bloodbraid-challenger) - is there anything better? Two-for-ones and disruption all the way down.

There was a sweet match that revolved around Archon answering their [Blightsteel](https://scryfall.com/card/2xm/235/blightsteel-colossus). The most "play to your outs" move was chump attacking [Inti](https://scryfall.com/card/lci/156/inti-seneschal-of-the-sun) to discard the Archon and exiled the [Animate Dead](https://scryfall.com/card/soc/207/animate-dead).

# Trophy Grixis Reanimator
![Trophy Grixis Reanimator](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-grixis-reanimator.png)

Yet another reanimator deck, but with blue this time!

Looking at it, I wouldn't expect this one to trophy. We're thin on the reanimation pieces - especially the looting/discard to fill the bin and dig through the deck - and our "fair" gameplan doesn't present a very strong threat.  Still, [Ancestral Recall](https://scryfall.com/card/vma/1/ancestral-recall) smoothes all draws!

New card [Stingcaster](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/93/stingcaster-mage) has been serviceable. Though it's a good step down from Snap, for sure, the haste is relevant and losing flash doesn't matter if re-casting reanimate or a hand attack.

# Trophy Boros & Boo
![Trophy Boros & Boo](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-boros-boo.png)

A fairly by-the-numbers Boros deck splashing over for Minsc & Boo & Bill.

I was really waffling around whether to go harder on green and play the mana dorks, but I opted for consistent threats. With acceleration, the curve's a bit high, but we do have both pitch elementals.

I don't think [Brightglass Gearhulk](https://scryfall.com/card/dft/191/brightglass-gearhulk) has ever actually made the cut, but with [Guide](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/29/guide-of-souls)/[Pride](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/38/ocelot-pride) this is definitely the deck. Maybe next season!

# Trophy Dimir Tempo
![Trophy Dimir tempo](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/trophy-dimir-tempo.png)

We really high rolled on this one. I was angling for a reanimator deck (as usual), but slumped over to a sordid tempo deck when that didn't come together.

Even looking at it now, I don't think this should've been a trophy deck - we just don't have many good threats! Still, a little disruption and a lot of luck got us there.

New card [Sphinx of False Conclusions](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/40/sphinx-of-false-conclusions) has been better than expect. Not a high pick, but it hits hard, frequently nets a two-for-one, and lets you hold up mana for a counterspell. This is kind of a perfect deck for it, where we're straddling tempo and reanimator.

# 7-2 4c Timewalk Slop
![Timewalk Slop](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-2-4c-timewalk-slop.png)

Switching over to Arena, where we didn't pick up many wins. This deck sure was [Timewalk](https://scryfall.com/card/vma/2/time-walk) and a pile of cards. Taking infinite turns with [Emeritus](https://scryfall.com/card/sos/145/emeritus-of-abundance-regrowth) is pretty slick though.

A lot of my drafts gravitate around the manabase. There's no shortage of busted cards, so the fixing tends to decide what actually makes the deck. Drafting on Arena really muddies that signal though, since the drafters don't prioritize duals, so I end up swimming in multicolour slop.

Though [Simulacrum Shaper](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/113/simulacrum-shaper) doesn't quite meet the bar for vintage cube, it's still a satisfying card. I might pick it up for my lower power cube, though I think [Sad Robot](https://scryfall.com/card/mrd/245/solemn-simulacrum) ultimately still wins out on cachet.

# 7-0 4c Control
![4c Control](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-0-4c-lotus-control.png)

More slop for the trough!

Okay, this one isn't so bad - we've got a reasonably focused controlling gameplan, enabled by three pieces of power. No counterspells, but a turn one [Teferi](https://scryfall.com/card/dom/207/teferi-hero-of-dominaria) lcoks things up well enough.

As usual, you can see the nod to reanimator - a few generally playable pieces and some castable threats. [Occult Epiphany](https://scryfall.com/card/frc/43/occult-epiphany) is a great include for the cube - fair, but strong and it has a lot of play to it.

# 7-2 Sultai Pacifism
![Sultai Pacifism](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-2-sultai-no-killspells.png)

Kind of a goofy deck - our only outright kill spells are [Lich's Relic](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/57/lichs-relic) and maybe [Grist](https://scryfall.com/card/dsc/220/grist-the-hunger-tide). We did [Oko](https://scryfall.com/card/eld/197/oko-thief-of-crowns) an Emrakrul though!

Once again, power saving our bacon here. Chucking out a turn two planeswalker will get the job done.

There's a small graveyard theme here, in part with new-to-the-cube [Jet Collector](https://scryfall.com/card/mbc/69/jet-collector). _Personally_ I think this one's a fine alchemy card - it's really not doing anything too funky, especially now that "real" cards [are making lotuses](https://scryfall.com/search?q=o%3Alotus+o%3Acreate&unique=cards&as=grid&order=name). Golgari could always use some help and I think this is a nice get.

# 7-2 Sultai Superfriends
![Sultai Superfriends](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-2-sultai-timewalk-superfriends.png)

Another timewalk deck, though more similar to the previous Sultai brew.

We've got four whole planeswalkers, not to mention some solid attackers, so we're set up well to capitalize on an extra turn, even if we're not abusing it.

Still undecided on [The Theorist](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/43/the-theorist-jace-beleren). I think it's decent and all told, an upgrade to [The Mind Scultpor](https://scryfall.com/card/2xm/56/jace-the-mind-sculptor) since you get card draw while afffecting the board. On the other hand, it doesn't have a ton of loyalty, especially if you need to bounce something. Fine, but no Minsc & Boo?

# 7-1 Golgari Nadu
![Golgari Nadu](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-1-golgari-nadu.png)

Yet another Sultai deck! This one is basically just Golgari with a cheeky splash for [Nadu](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/193/nadu-winged-wisdom) and [Oko](https://scryfall.com/card/eld/197/oko-thief-of-crowns).

Nadu's not at its best, but playable enough with [Bill](https://scryfall.com/card/otj/157/bristly-bill-spine-sower) and [Safekeeper](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/287/sylvan-safekeeper). The latter pairs with the [Baloth](https://scryfall.com/card/eoc/13/baloth-prime) and we were mostly just generating value until we could close out the game with a bunch of beasts.

Also not at its best here, [Carnivorous Cultivator](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/100/carnivorous-cultivator-enroot) is a solid addition to the cube. Beefy stats, strip locks, and even just abusing [Otawara](https://scryfall.com/card/neo/271/otawara-soaring-city). Nothing busted, but solid.

# 7-1 Mardu Agro
![Mardu Agro](/images/blogs/fra-cube-drafts/7-1-mardu-agro.png)

I was trying real hard not to land in Boros, but you can definitely feel the pull on this deck.

This was great! Tons of two-for-ones, pressure, and disruption, all powered out off a [Mana Crypt](https://scryfall.com/card/2xm/270/mana-crypt). Just about perfect.

[Phlage](https://scryfall.com/card/mh3/197/phlage-titan-of-fires-fury) is such a messed up card, especially in a deck like this that can loot her way for later. This deck's flaw is the manabase, but we can churn through enough to make RRWW an inevitable finisher.

# Farewell to FRA

And that wraps us up! On the whole, a really great set. [Cultivator](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/100/carnivorous-cultivator-enroot), [Stingcaster](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/93/stingcaster-mage), [big green Garruk](https://scryfall.com/card/fra/259/garruk-curse-breaker) - I love big green Garruk!

They've also been working hard to flesh out the Arena cube with cards like [Through the Breach](https://scryfall.com/card/inr/175/through-the-breach), [Exhume](https://scryfall.com/card/jmp/230/exhume), and [Oath](https://scryfall.com/card/c16/159/oath-of-druids), which is appreciated. I don't think Arena needs to exactly match MTGO - I enjoy it being a half step lower in power and open to some "cool" cards - but it helps a lot to get redundancy for these effects. 

On the whole, Reality Fracture has been phenomenal. It's great to close out the year, and our time with Jace, on such a high note and - wait, what do you mean "Star Trek"?